1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:04,701
Elasticsearch is a search server that runs apart from your site.

2
00:00:04,701 --> 00:00:09,247
In this video, you enable the Orchard Core Elasticsearch module, create an index

3
00:00:09,247 --> 00:00:13,001
of your articles on the server, and use that index for the search

4
00:00:13,001 --> 00:00:14,270
page of your site.

5
00:00:18,870 --> 00:00:22,819
The server can be a cloud service, an installation on your own machines,

6
00:00:22,819 --> 00:00:25,654
or a Docker container for development and testing.

7
00:00:25,654 --> 00:00:28,186
The Orchard Core module does not install it:

8
00:00:28,186 --> 00:00:30,920
it connects to a server that is already running.

9
00:00:31,670 --> 00:00:36,402
The connection belongs to the configuration of the site, so whoever hosts the

10
00:00:36,402 --> 00:00:40,051
site sets it, with the server address and any credentials.

11
00:00:40,051 --> 00:00:41,818
No admin screen edits it.

12
00:00:41,818 --> 00:00:46,550
Without a valid address, the menu entry Run Elasticsearch Query stays hidden.

13
00:00:47,300 --> 00:00:50,790
On the server, an index holds a copy of the content of the

14
00:00:50,790 --> 00:00:55,043
types you choose, and Orchard Core updates it when that content changes.

15
00:00:55,043 --> 00:00:58,096
The search page of your site reads from the index.

16
00:00:58,096 --> 00:01:01,150
The Lucene module can run alongside Elasticsearch.

17
00:01:05,346 --> 00:01:10,016
Let's open Tools, Features and filter the list for Elasticsearch.

18
00:01:10,016 --> 00:01:17,251
It shows Elasticsearch, Elasticsearch (Obsolete), Elasticsearch Worker and Elasticsearch Content Picker.

19
00:01:17,251 --> 00:01:21,526
Of these four, Elasticsearch is the one this video enables.

20
00:01:21,986 --> 00:01:24,529
Elasticsearch is the module itself.

21
00:01:24,529 --> 00:01:29,864
Elasticsearch (Obsolete) is kept for backwards compatibility, so leave it alone.

22
00:01:29,864 --> 00:01:34,206
Let's select Enable on Elasticsearch, and a message confirms it.

23
00:01:34,661 --> 00:01:38,743
The Search feature provides frontend search capabilities against indexes.

24
00:01:38,743 --> 00:01:42,721
Let's enable it too, because the search page of the site comes from it.

25
00:01:43,175 --> 00:01:49,667
With the module enabled and the connection valid, Search, Queries lists Run Elasticsearch Query.

26
00:01:49,667 --> 00:01:54,885
Its presence shows that the site configuration holds a valid server address.

27
00:01:59,097 --> 00:02:01,099
Let's open Search, Indexes.

28
00:02:01,099 --> 00:02:05,710
The page lists every index of the site, whatever its provider, and Add

29
00:02:05,710 --> 00:02:08,197
index offers the available sources.

30
00:02:08,647 --> 00:02:10,988
The sources are grouped by provider.

31
00:02:10,988 --> 00:02:16,505
Under Elasticsearch, Content in Elasticsearch creates an index from the content of your site.

32
00:02:16,505 --> 00:02:17,787
Let's choose Add.

33
00:02:18,275 --> 00:02:22,302
Index Name is the technical name of the index, in lowercase, and on

34
00:02:22,302 --> 00:02:25,225
the server the tenant name goes in front of it.

35
00:02:25,225 --> 00:02:29,086
Name is the label the admin shows, and it follows the Index Name

36
00:02:29,086 --> 00:02:30,521
until you change it.

37
00:02:30,521 --> 00:02:31,955
Let's type articles.

38
00:02:32,415 --> 00:02:34,993
Content types lists what can be indexed.

39
00:02:34,993 --> 00:02:36,506
Let's select Article.

40
00:02:36,506 --> 00:02:42,445
Culture stays on Any culture, and without Index Latest version, only published versions are indexed.

41
00:02:42,905 --> 00:02:46,634
Analyzer picks how Elasticsearch breaks text into terms.

42
00:02:46,634 --> 00:02:52,108
Its list holds the analyzers defined for the site, and standard when none is defined.

43
00:02:52,108 --> 00:02:56,620
Store Source Data keeps the fields of each document in the index, and

44
00:02:56,620 --> 00:02:58,485
it is checked by default.

45
00:02:58,950 --> 00:03:03,477
Default Query Settings control how the search page queries this index.

46
00:03:03,477 --> 00:03:08,719
Search type starts as Multi-Match Query (Default), and Query field names lists the

47
00:03:08,719 --> 00:03:12,651
fields to search, with the full-text field already selected.

48
00:03:12,651 --> 00:03:14,260
We keep these values.

49
00:03:14,793 --> 00:03:20,022
Save creates the index on the Elasticsearch server and fills it in the background.

50
00:03:20,022 --> 00:03:25,903
The list shows the index, with badges for its provider, Elasticsearch, and its type, Content.

51
00:03:30,087 --> 00:03:34,100
The search page of the site queries the default search index.

52
00:03:34,100 --> 00:03:39,731
Under Settings, Search, Site Search, Default search index lists the indexes of the site.

53
00:03:39,731 --> 00:03:41,947
Let's choose articles and save.

54
00:03:42,422 --> 00:03:46,962
Let's open the search page, at /search on the site, and search for harvest.

55
00:03:47,420 --> 00:03:51,650
The page asks Elasticsearch for matching content items and lists them.

56
00:03:51,650 --> 00:03:55,712
Only articles appear, since the index covers only the Article type.

57
00:03:55,712 --> 00:04:00,330
An article that you publish later is added to the index when it is published.

58
00:04:01,490 --> 00:04:05,654
Visitors who are not signed in can search only when their role may

59
00:04:05,654 --> 00:04:11,090
query the index, through the permission named Query any index or Query 'articles' Index.

60
00:04:15,290 --> 00:04:19,622
Each Elasticsearch index adds two entries to its Actions menu.

61
00:04:19,622 --> 00:04:24,337
Query Index runs a query against the index, and Index Info shows how

62
00:04:24,337 --> 00:04:27,840
Elasticsearch describes the index, for reference.

63
00:04:28,590 --> 00:04:34,607
Search, Queries, Run Elasticsearch Query opens a query screen for any Elasticsearch index.

64
00:04:34,607 --> 00:04:41,000
It takes queries in the Elasticsearch query format, and its results list the documents it finds.

65
00:04:41,750 --> 00:04:45,326
The pages that edit a part of a content type, or a field

66
00:04:45,326 --> 00:04:48,902
of a part, have an Elasticsearch Index settings section.

67
00:04:48,902 --> 00:04:53,343
Its checkbox, Include this element in the index, adds the value of that

68
00:04:53,343 --> 00:04:54,900
element to the index.

69
00:04:55,650 --> 00:05:01,499
The other Actions entries, Synchronize, Reset, Rebuild and Force Delete, work the same

70
00:05:01,499 --> 00:05:05,250
for every provider, and a separate video covers them.

71
00:05:07,250 --> 00:05:11,430
Connect the site, enable the module, create an index of your content, and

72
00:05:11,430 --> 00:05:13,335
choose it for the search page.

73
00:05:13,335 --> 00:05:14,710
Thanks for watching.

